```{r setup, include=FALSE}
library(wsMed)
options(width = 80)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  cache = FALSE,
  fig.width = 7,
  fig.height = 4.5
)
```

Start by reading the default report. Use more specific queries only when you
need a different effect, reporting scale or confidence level.

The example below is the parallel mediation model from [Get started](GetStarted.html).

<details>
<summary>Example setup</summary>

```{r fit}
data(example_data)
result <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2"
)
```

</details>

## Read the default report

```{r default-report}
print(result)
```

`print()` displays the core effects and analysis information. The report gives
the estimate, SE and CI for each specific indirect effect, the total indirect
effect, the direct effect and the total effect. Here the default output is
unstandardized, with 95% Monte Carlo CIs.

## Get a statistical summary

```{r default-summary}
summary(result)
```

`summary()` returns a structured report containing effects, path coefficients
and analysis settings; its default display shows the statistical tables.
Unlike `print()`, it creates a summary object that can be saved and reused:

```{r saved-summary}
s <- summary(result)
```

Typing `s` displays the same summary. Neither operation repeats fitting or
sampling. The stored tables retain full precision even though the printed
values are rounded.

## Plot the results

```{r default-plot}
plot(result)
```

The default plot of this model shows the specific indirect effects. To plot
all effects selected in the summary, use `plot(s)`. Both inherit the reporting
scale saved during the analysis. See the [plot gallery](PlottingEffects.html)
for the available displays and optional settings.

## Choose more specific output

Start with the calls above. The following options change one part of the report
without fitting again.

### Request standardized effects

```{r standardized-report}
summary(
  result,
  standardized = TRUE
)
```

For complete condition effects, standardization uses the model-implied marginal
SD of the outcome difference. See [standardized effects](StandardizedModeratedMediation.html)
for the treatment of paths, interactions and conditional effects.

If the original call used `standardized = TRUE`, plain `print(result)`,
`summary(result)` and `plot(result)` already use standardized output.

### Select an effect family

```{r select}
summary(
  result,
  type = "indirect"
)
```

Other selections include `"direct"`, `"total_indirect"`, `"total"`, `"paths"`
and `"parameters"`. Use `terms` only when selecting particular named effects.

### Change the confidence level

```{r confidence-level}
summary(
  result,
  level = 0.90
)
```

The interval is recalculated from the stored draws. Effect SEs are sample SDs
of the joint effect draws, and effect CIs are percentile intervals.

For a model that stored both inference engines, choose `method = "mc"` or
`method = "bootstrap"` in `summary()`. The default `print()` reports both.

### Choose moderator values

For moderated mediation, the default report uses the centering reference for
a continuous moderator and all observed levels for a categorical moderator.
Use `at` to choose raw values or category labels. For example, after fitting
the second [Get started example](GetStarted.html#example-2-add-a-continuous-moderator):

```{r probes, eval=FALSE}
summary(
  moderated,
  at = list(D3 = mean(example_data$D3))
)
```

These are conditional effects at specified values, not effects averaged over
the moderator distribution.

## Compare two pathways

A difference between effects needs its own CI. Use the stored joint draws to
form that difference, then print the result directly:

```{r contrast}
effects <- wsmed_effects(result)
difference <- wsmed_contrasts(
  effects,
  list(B_minus_A = c(indirect_2 = 1, indirect_1 = -1))
)
print(difference)
```

`B_minus_A` is the indirect effect through B minus the effect through A. Do not
subtract separate CI endpoints, or infer a difference simply because only one
of the separate intervals excludes zero.

## Extract or export results when needed

Extraction is useful for further calculations or export; it is not required
for ordinary reports and plots.

| Task | Call |
|---|---|
| Effects as a data frame | `as.data.frame(s)` |
| Selected result columns | `effect_table[, columns]` |
| Effect estimates | `coef(s)` |
| Effect CIs | `confint(s)` |
| Covariance of effect draws | `vcov(s)` |
| Path-coefficient table | `s$coefficients` |
| Method, scale and confidence level | `s$info` |
| Sample size of the fitted data | `nobs(result)` |

The methods on `s` concern its selected effects. In contrast, `coef(result)`,
`vcov(result)` and `confint(result)` concern primitive model parameters.

To choose which columns to display, list their names in the order you want.
For example, keep the effect name, estimate, SE and both CI limits:

```{r select-columns}
effect_table <- as.data.frame(s)
columns <- c(
  "label",
  "estimate",
  "std.error",
  "conf.low",
  "conf.high"
)
effect_table[, columns]
```

Remove `"std.error"` from `columns` to omit SEs, or remove `"conf.low"` and
`"conf.high"` to omit CIs. `names(effect_table)` lists all available columns;
for moderated mediation, retain `moderator` and `at` to identify the evaluated
values or groups. Use `s$coefficients[, columns]` for the path-coefficient table.

To export the same selected columns:

```{r export, eval=FALSE}
write.csv(
  effect_table[, columns],
  "mediation-effects.csv",
  row.names = FALSE
)

p <- plot(result)
ggplot2::ggsave(
  "mediation-effects.png",
  plot = p,
  width = 7,
  height = 4.5,
  dpi = 300
)
```

The CSV contains the selected columns at full precision. Use `as.data.frame(s)`
instead to export the complete effect table, including analysis settings.
Choose your own filenames when saving results.

**Next:** [customize a plot](PlottingEffects.html), or
[reuse a fit](ModularWorkflow.html) for another inference method.
